The '''2020 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League]], the 65th season of Europe's premier club football tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 28th season since it was re-branded from the [[European Champion Clubs' Cup]] to the [[UEFA Champions League]]. It was played on 23 August 2020 at the [[Estádio da Luz]] in [[Lisbon]], Portugal, between French club [[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]], in their first European Cup final, and German club [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] having returned to the final for the first time since [[2013 UEFA Champions League final|2013]]. The match was held [[Behind closed doors (sport)|behind closed doors]] due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Europe]].<ref name="uefa behind closed doors" />

Originally, it had been scheduled to be played at the [[Atatürk Olympic Stadium]] in [[Istanbul]], Turkey, on 30 May 2020.<ref name="auto">{{Cite news |date=23 March 2020 |title=UEFA Club Finals postponed |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025b-0f8e76c8c787-b9f310787e70-1000--uefa-club-finals-postponed/ |access-date=23 March 2020}}</ref> On 17 June 2020, the UEFA Executive Committee chose to relocate the final to Lisbon as part of a "final-eight tournament" consisting of single-match knockout ties played in two stadiums across the city.<ref name="ExCo 17 June">{{Cite news |date=17 June 2020 |title=UEFA competitions to resume in August |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025e-0fb6101cfdbf-8b878372d79d-1000--uefa-competitions-to-resume-in-august/ |access-date=17 June 2020}}</ref> The match was the first European premier tournament final to be played on a Sunday, and the first since [[2009 UEFA Champions League final|2009]] to not be played on a Saturday. It was also the first final of the competition to be played after June.

Bayern Munich won the final 1–0 thanks to a 59th-minute goal scored by former Paris Saint-Germain player [[Kingsley Coman]] assisted by Joshua Kimmich, who was later selected as [[man of the match]].<ref name="MOTM" /> Bayern secured their sixth European Cup title and second [[Treble (association football)#Continental trebles|continental treble]], becoming the second European men's football team to win the continental treble twice.<ref>{{cite web |date=4 September 2020 |title=Who's won the treble? Bayern double up |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0251-0e99b1ba85da-ec8053dc0a29-1000--who-s-won-the-treble-bayern-double-up/|access-date=8 October 2020 |website=[[UEFA]]}}</ref> Bayern also became the first team to claim any European competition [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#Ranking main European club competitions' winning club sides by winning percentage|with a 100% winning record]].<ref name="BBC final">{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=23 August 2020 |title=Paris St-Germain 0–1 Bayern Munich: German side win Champions League final |work=[[BBC Sport]] |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53867676 |access-date=23 August 2020}}</ref> As winners, they earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2019–20 UEFA Europa League]], [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]], in the [[2020 UEFA Super Cup]], and also qualified for the [[2020 FIFA Club World Cup]] in Qatar; Bayern went on to win both and complete a historic [[Sextuple (association football)|sextuple]] (six trophies in a year).<ref>{{cite news |date=11 February 2021 |title=Bayern pick up their sixth title in one year |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2021/02/fifa-club-world-cup/bayern-win-their-sixth-title-in-1-year |access-date=16 February 2021 |publisher=FC Bayern Munich}}</ref>

==Venue==
{{for|the details on the selection of Istanbul as the original host for the final|2021 UEFA Champions League final#Host selection}}
[[File:LuzLissabon.jpg|thumb|upright=1.4|The [[Estádio da Luz]] in [[Lisbon]] hosted the final.]]
The final was originally scheduled to be played at the [[Atatürk Olympic Stadium]] in [[Istanbul]], Turkey, on 30 May 2020.<ref name="venue">{{Cite news |date=24 May 2018 |title=Istanbul to host 2020 UEFA Champions League Final |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0245-0f8e60d11f0f-78765b792753-1000--istanbul-to-host-2020-uefa-champions-league-final/ |access-date=24 May 2018}}</ref> However, UEFA announced on 23 March 2020 that the final was postponed due to the COVID-19 pandemic.<ref name="auto"/> On 17 June 2020, the UEFA Executive Committee chose to relocate the final to Lisbon as part of a "final-eight tournament" consisting of single-match knockout ties played in two stadiums across the city.<ref name="ExCo 17 June"/> The match was the first European Cup/Champions League final to be played on a Sunday and the first since [[2009 UEFA Champions League final|2009]] to not be played on a Saturday. It was also the first final of the competition to be played after June.

The UEFA Executive Committee chose the [[Estádio da Luz]], officially known as the Estádio do Sport Lisboa e Benfica, in [[Lisbon]] as the final venue at their meeting on 17 June 2020.<ref name="ExCo 17 June" /> This is the second UEFA Champions League final hosted at the stadium; the first was in [[2014 UEFA Champions League final|2014]], when [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] secured their 10th title by beating [[Atlético Madrid]] 4–1 in the first final between [[Madrid derby|teams from the same city]].

The home stadium of Portuguese Primeira Liga side [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] since 2003, it was newly built to host five matches of [[UEFA Euro 2004]], including the [[UEFA Euro 2004 Final|final]]. Before its demolition in 2003, to make way for the new 65,000-capacity ground, the [[Estádio da Luz (1954)|original Estádio da Luz]] hosted the [[1992 European Cup Winners' Cup final]], where [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] beat [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] 2–0, and the second leg of the [[1983 UEFA Cup final]], where [[R.S.C. Anderlecht|Anderlecht]] secured a 1–1 draw with Benfica to lift the trophy.<ref name="previous finals">{{Cite web |date=1 April 2014 |title=Finals in Lisbon |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2086381.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140502114613/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2086381.html |archive-date=2 May 2014 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

Lisbon had also staged a European Cup final in [[1967 European Cup final|1967]], when Scottish side [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] beat [[Inter Milan]] of Italy 2–1 at the [[Estádio Nacional]]. The Portuguese capital also hosted the [[2005 UEFA Cup final]] at the [[Estádio José Alvalade]], home of Benfica's local rivals and finalists [[Sporting CP]], who lost 3–1 to [[PFC CSKA Moscow|CSKA Moscow]].<ref name="previous finals" />

==Background==
[[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]] (PSG) reached their first European Cup/Champions League final, becoming the fifth finalist representing France and the 41st overall. They entered the final having played 110 prior matches in the European Cup and Champions League, the most for a final debutant, surpassing [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]]'s record of 90 matches prior to their final debut in [[2006 UEFA Champions League final|2006]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Azzoni |first=Tales |date=18 August 2020 |title=PSG beats Leipzig 3–0 to reach 1st Champions League final |agency=[[Associated Press]] |url=https://apnews.com/a790952f9adf5171c3fbe1520072cfcf |access-date=19 August 2020}}</ref> The match was the seventh final to feature a French team, and the first since Monaco in [[2004 UEFA Champions League final|2004]]. [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] were the only French club to have won the competition, doing so in [[1993 UEFA Champions League final|1993]].<ref name="RSSSF EC">{{Cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=2 April 2020 |title=European Champions' Cup |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec1.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110208171501/http://rsssf.com/tablese/ec1.html |archive-date=8 February 2011 |access-date=18 August 2020 |website=[[RSSSF]]}}</ref> The match was the fourth time Paris Saint-Germain have appeared in the final of a UEFA competition, having previously appeared in two consecutive finals of the [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]]. The club won the [[1996 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup Final|1996 final]] 1–0 against [[SK Rapid Wien|Rapid Wien]], before losing 1–0 against [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] while attempting to defend their title in [[1997 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup Final|1997]].<ref name="ECWC">{{Cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=26 January 2000 |title=European Cup Winners' Cup |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec2.html |access-date=18 August 2020 |website=RSSSF}}</ref> PSG also featured in the [[1996 UEFA Super Cup]], losing 9–2 on aggregate to [[Juventus FC|Juventus]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=5 September 2019 |title=European Super Cup |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tabless/sup.html |access-date=18 August 2020 |website=RSSSF}}</ref> Paris were attempting to become the first French team to complete a continental treble, having won [[2019–20 Ligue 1|Ligue 1]], which was awarded to them based on PPG ratio as the season was ended prematurely due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in France]],<ref>{{Cite news |date=30 April 2020 |title=Ligue 1: Paris St-Germain awarded French title as season finished early |work=[[BBC Sport]] |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/52484926 |access-date=18 August 2020}}</ref> and the [[2019–20 Coupe de France|Coupe de France]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=24 July 2020 |title=Paris St-Germain 1–0 Saint-Etienne: PSG win French Cup despite Kylian Mbappe injury |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53532177 |access-date=18 August 2020}}</ref> The club also won the final season of the [[2019–20 Coupe de la Ligue|Coupe de la Ligue]] ([[league cup]]) and the [[2019 Trophée des Champions|Trophée des Champions]] ([[super cup]]),<ref>{{Cite news |date=31 July 2020 |title=Paris St-Germain beat Lyon in French League Cup final for another treble |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53617357 |access-date=18 August 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=3 August 2019 |title=PSG beat Rennes to win Trophee des Champions in China |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49221004 |access-date=18 August 2020}}</ref> thus winning all four domestic titles, though only the main domestic cup competition was considered for a continental treble.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Miller |first=Nick |date=5 June 2015 |title=Champions League treble: The seven clubs who claimed the prize |work=[[ESPN]] |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/uefa-champions-league/2/blog/post/2477324/champions-league-treble-the-seven-clubs-who-claimed-it |access-date=18 August 2020}}</ref>

In eighteen matches, Paris Saint-Germain had a record of eleven wins, two draws and five losses against German clubs in European competition. PSG won all four prior knockout ties in which they met German opposition, including earlier in the season against [[Borussia Dortmund]] in the round of 16 and [[RB Leipzig]] in the semi-finals.<ref name="press kit">{{Cite web |date=20 August 2020 |title=UEFA Champions League – 2019/20 season: Match press kits, Final |url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2020/e/e_2030150_pk.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200826162128/https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2020/e/e_2030150_pk.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=26 August 2020 |access-date=20 August 2020 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

[[File:Hans-Dieter Flick, Germany national football team (03).jpg|thumb|upright|Bayern Munich manager [[Hansi Flick]] appeared in his second European Cup final, having lost as a player with Bayern in [[1987 European Cup final|1987]].]]

[[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] reached their eleventh European Cup/Champions League final, tying [[AC Milan|Milan]] for the second-most finals behind Real Madrid's 16. Most recently they appeared in the [[2013 UEFA Champions League final|2013 final]], in which they won 2–1 against archrivals Borussia Dortmund for their fifth title. In their prior finals, Bayern Munich won on four other occasions in [[1974 European Cup final|1974]], [[1975 European Cup final|1975]], [[1976 European Cup final|1976]] and [[2001 UEFA Champions League final|2001]], and lost in [[1982 European Cup final|1982]], [[1987 European Cup final|1987]], [[1999 UEFA Champions League final|1999]], 2010 and [[2012 UEFA Champions League final|2012]].<ref name="RSSSF EC" /> The match was Bayern's 13th overall final in European competition, having won the [[1967 European Cup Winners' Cup Final]] 1–0 after extra time against [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] and the [[1996 UEFA Cup Final]] 5–1 on aggregate against [[FC Girondins de Bordeaux|Bordeaux]].<ref name="ECWC" /><ref name="UC">{{Cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=2 April 2020 |title=UEFA Cup |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec3b.html |access-date=19 August 2020 |website=[[RSSSF]]}}</ref> Bayern Munich were chasing a second treble in club history (previously doing so in [[2012–13 FC Bayern Munich season|2012–13]]), having won the [[2019–20 Bundesliga|Bundesliga]] and [[2019–20 DFB-Pokal|DFB-Pokal]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Johnston |first=Neil |date=16 June 2020 |title=Bayern Munich win eighth successive Bundesliga title after beating Werder Bremen |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53071043 |access-date=16 June 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Begley |first=Emlyn |date=4 July 2020 |title=Bayer Leverkusen 2–4 Bayern Munich: Robert Lewandowski double helps win German Cup |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53291688 |access-date=19 August 2020}}</ref> Entering the final, Bayern are on a 20-match winning streak and undefeated in 2020, having not lost in their prior 29 matches (winning 28 and drawing once).<ref name="BBC BAY SF">{{Cite news |last=Dawkes |first=Phil |date=19 August 2020 |title=Lyon 0–3 Bayern Munich: Bayern breeze through to final showdown with PSG |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53815521 |access-date=19 August 2020}}</ref> With their semi-final win, Bayern also tied the record for the most consecutive wins in the competition proper, as well as the most wins from the start of the competition proper, with 10.<ref>{{Cite news |date=19 August 2020 |title=Longest winning runs in the Champions League |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0260-1022b42e6217-608706466bcb-1000/ |access-date=20 August 2020}}</ref> Bayern are the second team to have entered the final with a perfect winning record, after Milan in 1993, who lost to the only French winners in competition history at that point, Marseille. Bayern are also the second club to reach the final after winning all six group stage matches, after the aforementioned Milan team in [[1992–93 UEFA Champions League|1992–93]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ross |first=James M. |date=16 July 2015 |title=Champions' Cup 1992–93 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/ec/ec199293.html |access-date=19 August 2020 |website=[[RSSSF]]}}</ref>

Bayern Munich scored 42 goals in 10 matches during the competition prior to the final, second only to Barcelona's record of 45 goals in 16 matches during the [[1999–2000 UEFA Champions League|1999–2000 season]]. However, Bayern set a new record for the best goals per game ratio in competition history following their quarter-final (regardless of the outcome of the remainder of the competition), with 4.2 per match after their semi-final victory.<ref>{{Cite news |date=19 August 2020 |title=Most goals in a Champions League season |work=UEFA |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0260-1022946a3df4-505a0487e598-1000/ |access-date=20 August 2020}}</ref> Entering the final, forward [[Robert Lewandowski]] scored 15 goals in 9 matches during the Champions League season, with only [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] having scored more in a single campaign (17 in [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League|2013–14]] and 16 in [[2015–16 UEFA Champions League|2015–16]]). Lewandowski also tied [[Ruud van Nistelrooy]]'s mark from 2002–03 of scoring in nine consecutive Champions League matches, second only to Ronaldo's 11 from 2017–18.<ref name="BBC BAY SF" /> Lewandowski and [[Serge Gnabry]] set a record for the most prolific scoring partnership in a season with 24 combined goals, surpassing the record of 23 between Ronaldo and [[Gareth Bale]] in 2013–14.<ref>{{Cite news |date=20 August 2020 |title=Bayern Munich's Serge Gnabry and Robert Lewandowski break Cristiano Ronaldo-Gareth Bale goals record |publisher=Bundesliga |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/lewandowski-and-gnabry-break-ronaldo-bale-goals-record-champions-league-12535 |access-date=20 August 2020}}</ref> Bayern manager [[Hansi Flick]] became the 15th individual to [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics#Final and winning records|appear in a Champions League final as both a player and manager]], having played for Bayern in their loss to [[FC Porto|Porto]] in the 1987 final.<ref name="finals">{{Cite web |year=2012 |title=European Champion Clubs' Cup – History: Finals |url=https://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/EuroExperience/competitions/Publications/01/88/50/84/1885084_DOWNLOAD.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130128170559/http://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/EuroExperience/competitions/Publications/01/88/50/84/1885084_DOWNLOAD.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=28 January 2013 |access-date=19 August 2020 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations }}</ref>

In 34 matches, Bayern Munich had a record of 19 wins, 5 draws and ten losses against French clubs in European competition. Bayern won six of their seven previous knockout ties against French opposition, two of which were finals, including their semi-final win over [[Olympique Lyonnais|Lyon]] to reach the 2020 final. Bayern's only loss was 3–2 on aggregate against [[AS Saint-Étienne|Saint-Étienne]] in the first round of the [[1969–70 European Cup]].<ref name="press kit" />

The final was the ninth meeting between Paris Saint-Germain and Bayern Munich, with a record of five wins for PSG and three wins for Bayern. The fixture was the first knockout match between the sides, with all their prior meetings occurring in the Champions League group stages. The sides most recently met in the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League group stage#Group B|2017–18 UEFA Champions League group stage]]; Paris won the first match 3–0 at home, while Bayern won the return match 3–1.<ref>{{Cite news |date=18 August 2020 |title=Champions League final head-to-head: Paris vs Bayern |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025f-0fd682a7776c-cf86f236346f-1000/ |access-date=19 August 2020}}</ref> As the sides were level on points, PSG won the group on head-to-head goal difference.

The match was the second European Cup final to feature a French and German team, after the 1976 final between Bayern and [[A.S. Saint-Étienne|Saint-Étienne]].<ref name="RSSSF EC" /> Overall, it was the fourth final in European competition between teams from France and Germany, also occurring in the 1992 European Cup Winners' Cup Final (between Werder Bremen and Monaco) and the 1996 UEFA Cup Final (between Bayern and Bordeaux). On all three occasions, German teams were victorious.<ref name="ECWC" /><ref name="UC" /> It was also the third final in which both teams entered the match seeking a win to secure the [[Treble (association football)|treble]], previously occurring in [[2010 UEFA Champions League final|2010]], in which Bayern lost to Inter Milan, and [[2015 UEFA Champions League final|2015]], when Barcelona defeated Juventus. The 1999 final, won by [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]], also had both finalists seeking a treble, though Bayern had not yet contested their domestic cup final (which they ultimately lost). Both sides came into the final having won their domestic leagues the previous season, the first time this had happened since the [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998 final]] between Juventus and Real Madrid.<ref>{{Cite news |date=19 August 2020 |title=Alphonso Davies, Bayern Munich cruise into 1st Champions League final in 7 years |work=[[Canadian Broadcasting Corporation]] |url=https://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/champions-league-bayern-munich-lyon-semifinal-1.5692768 |access-date=19 August 2020}}</ref> It was also the first time since [[1994 UEFA Champions League final|1994]] that both finalists won their domestic league in the previous and same season as reaching the final.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Karpati |first1=Tamas |last2=Kramarsic |first2=Igor |date=30 July 2020 |title=Italy – List of Champions |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesi/italchamp.html |access-date=20 August 2020 |website=RSSSF}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Carnicero |first1=Tejedor |last2=Vicente |first2=José |date=23 July 2020 |title=Spain – List of Champions |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tabless/spanchamp.html |access-date=20 August 2020 |website=RSSSF}}</ref> The final was also the second to feature two German managers, after the 2013 edition with managers [[Jürgen Klopp]] for Borussia Dortmund and [[Jupp Heynckes]] for Bayern Munich.<ref name="finals" />

