===Paris Saint-Germain===
{{see also|2019–20 Paris Saint-Germain F.C. season}}

[[File:PSG-Shakhter15 (2).jpg|thumb|upright|[[Ángel Di María]] of Paris Saint-Germain had the joint-most assists in the competition with six.<ref name="top scorers">{{Cite web |title=UEFA Champions League – Top Assists |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2020/statistics/round=2001140/players/kind=goals/index.html#order=4desc |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20200824193714/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2020/statistics/round=2001140/players/kind=goals/index.html%23order=4desc |archive-date=24 August 2020 |access-date=23 August 2020 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations }}</ref>]]

Paris Saint-Germain qualified for the Champions League group stage by winning the [[2018–19 Ligue 1]] season, their sixth title in seven seasons and eighth overall French title. In the group stage, they were drawn into Group A alongside Real Madrid of Spain, record winners of the European Cup, as well as [[Galatasaray S.K. (football)|Galatasaray]] of Turkey and [[Club Brugge KV|Club Brugge]] of Belgium.<ref name="GS draw">{{Cite magazine |last=Creditor |first=Avi |date=29 August 2019 |title=Champions League Group Stage Draw: Results for 2019–20 |magazine=[[Sports Illustrated]] |url=https://www.si.com/soccer/2019/08/29/champions-league-draw-group-stage-2019-20-results-pots |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

In the opening match of the group stage, Paris faced Real Madrid at the [[Parc des Princes]] and won 3–0 via a first-half brace from former Madrid player [[Ángel Di María]] and a second-half stoppage time goal from [[Thomas Meunier]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Sutcliffe |first=Steve |date=18 September 2019 |title=Champions League: Paris St-Germain 3–0 Real Madrid |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49732379 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> On matchday 2, PSG won 1–0 against Galatasaray at the [[Türk Telekom Stadium]] with a 52nd-minute goal from [[Mauro Icardi]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=1 October 2019 |title=Paris St-Germain 1–0 Galatasaray: Mauro Icardi scores first PSG goal in Champions League win |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49872942 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> Paris Saint-Germain continued their perfect start to the competition by defeating Club Brugge 5–0 at the [[Jan Breydel Stadium]], with a brace from Icardi and hat-trick from [[Kylian Mbappé]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=22 October 2019 |title=Club Bruges 0–5 Paris St-Germain: Kylian Mbappe dazzles with second-half hat-trick |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49874401 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> In their home meeting against Brugge, Icardi scored PSG's lone goal in a 1–0 win to advance to the knockout stage.<ref>{{Cite news |date=6 November 2019 |title=Paris St-Germain 1–0 Club Bruges: French champions maintain perfect start to qualify |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50296570 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> On matchday 5, Paris recovered from a two-goal deficit to draw 2–2 at the [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium|Santiago Bernabéu]] against Madrid and win Group A, with Mbappé and [[Pablo Sarabia]]'s late goals offsetting a [[Karim Benzema]] brace.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Begley |first=Emlyn |date=26 November 2019 |title=Real Madrid 2–2 Paris St-Germain: French side battle back to top Champions League group |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50549677 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> In their final match, Paris Saint-Germain won 5–0 at home against Galatasaray to finish the group stage undefeated and having only conceded two goals.<ref>{{Cite news |date=11 December 2019 |title=Paris St-Germain 5–0 Galatasaray: French champions maintain unbeaten record |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50749875 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

In the round of 16, PSG were drawn against German club Borussia Dortmund. In the first leg at the [[Westfalenstadion]], Paris lost 2–1 with [[Neymar]] scoring a goal in between a brace from [[Erling Haaland]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=18 February 2020 |title=Dortmund 2–1 Paris: Haaland gives BVB edge |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0e9f79b54fad-4ba85e60fe75-1000--dortmund-2-1-paris-haaland-gives-bvb-edge/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> The second leg was one of the last European matches played prior to the suspension of the competition due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], and took place [[Behind closed doors (sport)|behind closed doors]] on 11 March 2020. PSG won the match 2–0 with first-half goals from Neymar and [[Juan Bernat]], securing a 3–2 aggregate win for the Parisians and their first quarter-final appearance since the 2015–16 season.<ref>{{Cite news |date=11 March 2020 |title=Paris 2–0 Dortmund (agg: 3–2): Neymar leads Paris through |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025b-0ee7f29885e7-27d8bd71fbcc-1000--paris-2-0-dortmund-agg-3-2-neymar-leads-paris-through/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

Paris were drawn against Italian club [[Atalanta BC|Atalanta]] in the quarter-finals, who were participating in their debut season of the Champions League. The one-off tie was played on 12 August 2020 at the neutral Estádio da Luz. Atalanta took the lead in the first-half with a goal from [[Mario Pašalić]]. PSG struggled to find an equaliser in the second half, until [[Marquinhos]] scored from close range in the 90th minute before substitute [[Eric Maxim Choupo-Moting]] scored three minutes later to snatch a late win for Paris. Paris Saint-Germain advanced to the Champions League semi-finals for the second time in their history after the [[1994–95 UEFA Champions League|1994–95 season]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=12 August 2020 |title=Atalanta 1–2 Paris: Tuchel's side pull off stunning late comeback |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0260-101dac0c3e3f-d6915cd43ac5-1000--atalanta-1-2-paris-tuchel-s-side-pull-off-stunning-late-com/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

In the semi-finals, Paris Saint-Germain faced RB Leipzig of Germany, who had qualified to the Champions League knockout stage for the first time in only their second season competing in the competition. Marquinhos and Di María scored in the first half at Estádio da Luz, while Bernat scored 11 minutes into the second period to earn PSG a 3–0 win and a spot in their first Champions League final.<ref>{{Cite news |date=18 August 2020 |title=Leipzig 0–3 Paris: Tuchel's side reach first Champions League final |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0260-1029af22ae00-e7a42bc4728b-1000--leipzig-0-3-paris-tuchel-s-side-reach-first-champions-le/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

===Bayern Munich===
{{see also|2019–20 FC Bayern Munich season}}

[[File:Robert Lewandowski123666.jpg|thumb|upright=1|Bayern striker [[Robert Lewandowski]] was the competition's leading scorer and joint-leading assister with 15 goals and 6 assists en route to the final.<ref name="top scorers" />]]

Bayern Munich qualified directly for the group stage of the Champions League by winning the [[2018–19 Bundesliga]], their 7th consecutive and 29th overall German title. In the group stage, they were drawn into Group B alongside [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] of England, runners-up of the [[2018–19 UEFA Champions League|previous Champions League season]], as well as [[Olympiacos F.C.|Olympiacos]] of Greece and [[Red Star Belgrade]] of Serbia.<ref name="GS draw" /> The team began their season under Croatian manager [[Niko Kovač]].

In their first match, Bayern faced Red Star Belgrade in a home match at the [[Allianz Arena]] and won 3–0 with goals from [[Kingsley Coman]], Robert Lewandowski and [[Thomas Müller]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=18 September 2019 |title=Coman im Flug zur 16 – FC Bayern besiegt Roter Stern |language=de |trans-title=Coman header to 16 – FC Bayern defeats Red Star |work=[[Kicker (sports magazine)|kicker]] |url=https://www.kicker.de/bayern-gegen-roter-stern-2019-champions-league-4612103/analyse |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> In their second fixture against Tottenham at [[Tottenham Hotspur Stadium]], Bayern won 7–2 with a goal from [[Joshua Kimmich]], a brace from Lewandowski and four goals scored by Serge Gnabry.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |author-link=Phil McNulty |date=1 October 2019 |title=Tottenham 2–7 Bayern Munich: Serge Gnabry scores four in Champions League demolition |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49872949 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> The result was the heaviest home loss by an English club in European competition.<ref>{{Cite news |date=1 October 2019 |title=Tottenham Hotspur F.C. in Champions League capitulation as FC Bayern Munich wins 7–2 |work=[[USA Today]] |agency=[[Associated Press]] |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/europe/2019/10/01/champions-league-tottenham-has-humiliating-loss-bayern-munich/3836144002/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> On matchday 3, Bayern faced Olympiacos at the [[Karaiskakis Stadium]] and recovered from a deficit to win 3–2 and maintain their lead in the group with a brace from Lewandowski followed by a curled goal from [[Corentin Tolisso]], while [[Youssef El-Arabi]] and [[Guilherme dos Santos Torres|Guilherme]] scored for the Greek side.<ref>{{Cite news |date=22 October 2019 |title=Robert Lewandowski: Is the Bayern Munich player the world's best striker right now? |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50145108 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

However, as Bayern lost 5–1 to [[Eintracht Frankfurt]] in the [[Bundesliga]] on 2 November, manager Niko Kovač was sacked the following day and replaced by former assistant Hansi Flick on an interim basis.<ref>{{Cite news |date=3 November 2019 |title=Bayern München trennt sich von Trainer Kovac |language=de |trans-title=Bayern Munich separates from manager Kovač |work=[[Kicker (sports magazine)|kicker]] |url=https://www.kicker.de/bayern_muenchen_trennt_sich_von_trainer_kovac-761680/artikel |access-date=3 November 2019}}</ref> In Flick's first match in charge three days later, Bayern won the rematch against Olympiacos 2–0 via second-half goals from Lewandowski and [[Ivan Perišić]], thus securing a spot in the knockout stage.<ref>{{Cite news |date=6 November 2019 |title=Bayern Munich 2–0 Olympiakos: Robert Lewandowski helps seal last-16 spot |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50296535 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> On 26 November, Bayern Munich won 6–0 against Red Star Belgrade at the [[Red Star Stadium]] to win Group B, with Lewandowski scoring four times between goals from [[Leon Goretzka]] and Tolisso.<ref>{{Cite news |date=27 November 2019 |title=Red Star Belgrade 0–6 Bayern Munich: Robert Lewandowski scores four |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50567024 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> In their final match, goals from Coman, Müller and [[Philippe Coutinho]] earned Bayern a 3–1 home win against Tottenham Hotspur to finish with a perfect group stage record, while [[Ryan Sessegnon]] scored the only goal for the visitors.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rose |first=Gary |date=11 December 2019 |title=Bayern Munich 3–1 Tottenham Hotspur: Ryan Sessegnon scores on full debut as Spurs lose |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50719836 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> This was the seventh occasion a team finished the group stage with six wins from six, with Bayern the sixth club to do so. Following a successful spell as interim manager, Bayern announced on 22 December that Flick would remain manager until the end of season.<ref>{{Cite news |date=22 December 2019 |title=Flick to remain FC Bayern head coach until end of season |publisher=FC Bayern Munich |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2019/12/hansi-flick-to-remain-fc-bayern-head-coach-until-end-of-season |access-date=26 December 2019}}</ref>

Bayern were drawn against English club [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in the round of 16, with the last Champions League meeting between the sides taking place in the 2012 final. In the first leg away at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]], Bayern won 3–0 via a brace from Gnabry followed by a tap-in from Lewandowski set-up by [[Alphonso Davies]]. Chelsea defender [[Marcos Alonso (footballer, born 1990)|Marcos Alonso]] was sent off late in the match, which was the club's heaviest home loss in European competition.<ref>{{Cite news |date=25 February 2020 |title=Chelsea 0–3 Bayern: Gnabry strikes twice |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ec9c963f0b5-782f0510f059-1000--chelsea-0-3-bayern-gnabry-strikes-twice/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> Following the match, Bayern chairman [[Karl-Heinz Rummenigge]] suggested Flick would stay permanently as manager.<ref>{{Cite news |date=25 February 2020 |title=Bayern CEO Rummenigge hints that Flick could stay on as manager into next season |work=[[Goal (website)|Goal]] |url=https://www.goal.com/en-us/news/bayern-ceo-rummenigge-hints-that-flick-could-stay-on/10j2n6l55zw3n1cjl4w3blxdz5 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> On 3 April, Flick signed a new contract with the club lasting until 2023.<ref>{{Cite news |date=3 April 2020 |title="Dauerhaft Cheftrainer": FC Bayern verlängert mit Flick bis 2023 |language=de |trans-title="Permanent head coach": FC Bayern extends until 2023 with Flick |work=kicker |url=https://www.kicker.de/_dauerhaft_cheftrainer_fc_bayern_verlaengert_mit_flick_bis_2023-771422/artikel |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> Due to the suspension of the competition due to the COVID-19 pandemic, the second leg was delayed from 18 March until 8 August 2020, and was played behind closed doors at the Allianz Arena. Bayern won the second meeting 4–1, with a Lewandowski brace and goals from Perišić and Tolisso earning a 7–1 victory on aggregate, while [[Tammy Abraham]] scored for the visitors.<ref>{{Cite news |date=8 August 2020 |title=Bayern 4–1 Chelsea (agg: 7–1): Lewandowski at the double |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0260-101599d04d8b-ab50b00312ef-1000--bayern-4-1-chelsea-agg-7-1-lewandowski-at-the-double/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> The result was Chelsea's heaviest defeat in a two-legged European tie.<ref>{{Cite news |date=8 August 2020 |title=Chelsea defence at its worst in 29 years after record aggregate defeat in Europe |work=[[Yahoo Sports]] |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/chelsea-defence-worst-29-years-220247512.html |access-date=24 August 2020 |archive-date=26 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200826162149/https://sports.yahoo.com/chelsea-defence-worst-29-years-220247512.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

As a result of the draw, Bayern faced Spanish club Barcelona in the quarter-finals. The match, played at the Estádio da Luz, finished as an [[FC Barcelona 2–8 FC Bayern Munich|8–2 win]] for Bayern Munich, with Perišić, Gnabry, Kimmich and Lewandowski scoring once each while Müller and Coutinho both earned braces. [[David Alaba]] scored an early own goal for Barcelona, who netted their only other goal via a [[Luis Suárez (Uruguayan footballer)|Luis Suárez]] strike in the 57th minute. For Bayern Munich, the result was the most goals scored by the club in a match during the Champions League era, and the second most for them in competition history after nine scored against [[AC Omonia|Omonia]] in the [[1972–73 European Cup]].<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Zea |first1=Antonio |last2=Haisma |first2=Marcel |date=9 January 2008 |title=Champions' Cup 1972–73 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/ec/ec197273det.html |access-date=24 August 2020 |website=[[RSSSF]]}}</ref> The match was the first time Barcelona conceded six goals or more in European competition, and the first time they conceded four goals in the first half of a Champions League match.<ref>{{Cite news |date=14 August 2020 |title=Barcelona 2–8 Bayern: record-breaking win for rampant Germans |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0260-1020a197123b-5848d2b10d39-1000--barcelona-2-8-bayern-record-breaking-win-for-rampant-germans/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> It was Barcelona's heaviest defeat since 1951, when they lost 6–0 to [[RCD Espanyol|Espanyol]] in the [[1950–51 La Liga]], and the first time they conceded eight goals in a game since 1946, when they lost 8–0 to [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]] in the [[1946 Copa del Generalísimo#Round of 16|1946 Copa del Generalísimo]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Azzoni |first=Tales |date=14 August 2020 |title=Bayern humbles Barcelona 8–2 to reach Champions League semis |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |agency=[[Associated Press]] |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/soccer/bayern-stuns-barcelona-8-2-to-reach-champions-league-semis/2020/08/14/af0b9cea-de70-11ea-b4f1-25b762cdbbf4_story.html |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

Bayern met French club Lyon in the semi-finals of the competition at the Estádio José Alvalade, a rematch of the [[2009–10 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Semi-finals|2009–10 semi-finals]]. Bayern won 3–0 with a brace from Gnabry followed by a late goal from Lewandowski to advance to their first Champions League final since 2013.<ref>{{Cite news |date=19 August 2020 |title=Lyon 0–3 Bayern: Gnabry leads Flick's men into final |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0260-102bbe96cbe4-17a26d865877-1000--lyon-0-3-bayern-gnabry-leads-flick-s-men-into-final/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

==Pre-match==
[[File:2020 UCL Final identity.jpg|thumb|right|180px|Original identity of the final.]]
[[File:Daniele Orsato 2016 (cropped).jpg|thumb|180px|Italian [[Daniele Orsato]] was the referee for the final.]]

===Identity===
The original identity of the 2020 UEFA Champions League final was unveiled at the group-stage draw on 29 August 2019.<ref>{{Cite web |date=29 August 2019 |title=UEFA Champions League launches 2020 Istanbul final identity |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0254-0f8e6eac1e46-2ec862941370-1000--uefa-champions-league-launches-2020-istanbul-final-identity/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

===Ambassador===
The ambassador for the original Istanbul final was former Turkish international [[Hamit Altıntop]],<ref>{{Cite news |date=1 May 2020 |title=EURO 2008 spotlight: How brilliant was Turkey's Hamit Altıntop? |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro-2020/news/025d-0f4d4e874249-706b086c7e61-1000--all-hail-hamit-altintop/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200506221312/https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro-2020/news/025d-0f4d4e874249-706b086c7e61-1000--all-hail-hamit-altintop/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=6 May 2020 |access-date=20 June 2020 |quote=...Turkish Football Federation's Executive Committee members planning the UEFA Champions League 2020 final in Istanbul. Hamit is a UEFA ambassador for the city.}}</ref> who finished as runner-up in the [[2009–10 UEFA Champions League]] with Bayern Munich as well as winning the [[2003 UEFA Intertoto Cup|2003]] and [[2004 UEFA Intertoto Cup]]s with [[FC Schalke 04|Schalke 04]].

===Officials===
On 20 August 2020, UEFA named Italian [[Daniele Orsato]] as the referee for the final. Orsato had been a [[FIFA International Referees List|FIFA referee]] since 2010, and was previously the fourth official in the [[2019 UEFA Europa League Final]]. He was also an assistant video assistant referee in the [[2018 FIFA World Cup Final]]. He was also an additional assistant referee at [[UEFA Euro 2016]] and a [[video assistant referee]] at the [[2018 FIFA World Cup]]. He was joined by four of his fellow countrymen, with Lorenzo Manganelli and Alessandro Giallatini as assistant referees, [[Massimiliano Irrati]] as the video assistant referee and [[Marco Guida]] as the assistant VAR official. The fourth official was [[Ovidiu Hațegan]] of Romania, while Spaniards Roberto Díaz Pérez del Palomar and [[Alejandro Hernández Hernández]] served as the offside and support VAR officials, respectively.<ref name="officials">{{Cite news |date=19 August 2020 |title=Referee team appointed for UEFA Champions League final in Lisbon |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0260-102d0728ce34-c1031f033e1e-1000/ |access-date=19 August 2020}}</ref>

===Team selection===
Each team made one change to their starting line-up following the semi-finals. After recovering from injury, Paris Saint-Germain first-choice goalkeeper [[Keylor Navas]] started in place of [[Sergio Rico]]. For Bayern Munich, manager Hansi Flick decided to replace Ivan Perišić, who had started in the three prior Champions League matches, with Paris native Kingsley Coman on the left wing. Centre-back [[Jérôme Boateng]] was also deemed fit to start for Bayern, having suffered a minor injury in the semi-final which required him to be substituted off at half-time.<ref name="ESPN">{{Cite news |date=23 August 2020 |title=Bayern Munich beat Paris Saint-Germain to win Champions League |work=[[ESPN]] |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report?gameId=573698 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
[[File:Kingsley Coman (2019) (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright=.8|Bayern winger [[Kingsley Coman]] scored the only goal of the match in the 59th minute.]]

Paris Saint-Germain kicked off the match, though Bayern Munich began quickly in the opening stages, before Paris found their way into the game after 10 minutes. In the 18th minute, PSG had their first clear opportunity when Kylian Mbappé passed to Neymar on the left side of the penalty area, but his shot was blocked by the leg of Bayern goalkeeper [[Manuel Neuer]]. In the 22nd minute, Bayern forward Robert Lewandowski received the ball in the penalty area, swiveling and shooting the ball towards the goal which deflected off the inside of the left post and back into play. Two minutes later, Ángel Di María found himself with space in front of goal after a one-two with [[Ander Herrera]], but shot the ball over the crossbar of Neuer's goal. During the sequence, Bayern defender Jérôme Boateng injured himself, and had to be replaced by [[Niklas Süle]]. Herrera had a drop-kick deflected wide of the Bayern goal in the 29th minute, while Lewandowski's 31st-minute dipping header was parried by PSG goalkeeper Keylor Navas. In the 45th minute, a misplaced pass by David Alaba in the Bayern back line fell in front of Mbappé, who played a one-two with Herrera before shooting the ball straight at Neuer when confronted by a clear opening on goal.<ref name="ESPN" /> In the following minute of first-half stoppage time, Kingsley Coman was hit on the shoulder by the arm of defender [[Thilo Kehrer]] and went down in the penalty area after cutting down the goal line. However, referee Daniele Orsato dismissed the Bayern appeals for a penalty, and blew for half-time with the match scoreless.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hytner |first=David |date=23 August 2020 |title=Bayern Munich win Champions League as Kingsley Coman header sinks PSG |work=[[The Guardian]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2020/aug/23/bayern-munich-paris-saint-germain-psg-champions-league-final-match-report |access-date=23 August 2020}}</ref>

In the second half, Bayern scored the only goal in the 59th minute with a header by Coman, a PSG academy graduate. Thomas Müller laid a pass from Serge Gnabry back to right-back Joshua Kimmich, who crossed the ball from the right into the box towards the unmarked Coman at the far post, which was headed across the goal past Navas and into the right corner of the net. Following the goal, Bayern quickly created additional opportunities in the Parisian half, but were unable to capitalise on the chances. Bayern subsequently switched to a more defensive approach, and in the 70th minute Neuer made another save with his leg after Marquinhos found himself clear on goal following a pass from Ángel Di María into the left side of the penalty box. Three minutes later, Mbappé was caught on the back of his foot by Kimmich after cutting inside the Bayern penalty area, but referee Orsato again decided to not award a penalty. The final chance of the match came in stoppage time, when Mbappé ran down the inside-left channel and passed to Neymar on the left side of Bayern's penalty box, who turned sharply and shot wide of the far post, with substitute Eric Maxim Choupo-Moting's outstretched leg failing to meet the ball directly in front of the goal.<ref name="ESPN" /> Minutes later, the match ended as a 1–0 win for Bayern, securing the continental treble.<ref>{{Cite news |date=23 August 2020 |title=Triple perfekt! Coman köpft Bayern auf Europas Thron |trans-title=Treble perfect! Coman heads Bayern to the European throne |work=[[Kicker (sports magazine)|kicker]] |url=https://www.kicker.de/paris-sg-gegen-bayern-2020-champions-league-4651902/analyse |access-date=23 August 2020}}</ref>

