==Post-match==
With the win, Bayern Munich secured their sixth European Cup title to draw level with [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]], behind only Real Madrid (15 titles) and Milan (7 titles). As Bayern also won the Bundesliga and DFB-Pokal, they secured a second continental treble after the 2012–13 season. It was the ninth treble won in Europe, with Bayern Munich only the second club to win it twice after Barcelona in [[2008–09 FC Barcelona season|2008–09]] and [[2014–15 FC Barcelona season|2014–15]].

Former Paris Saint-Germain player Kingsley Coman, who scored the winning goal for Bayern, earned the man of the match award.<ref name="MOTM" /> Bayern goalkeeper Manuel Neuer was voted as the Champions League player of the week, having made three saves during the match to keep a clean sheet.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 August 2020 |title=Player of the Week |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/player-of-the-week/ |archive-url=https://archive.today/20200828202029/https://uclpotw.uefa.com/%23/en/previous-winners#/en/ |archive-date=28 August 2020 |access-date=28 August 2020 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url-status=live }}</ref> Bayern became the first club to win any European competition [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#Ranking three main European club competitions' winning club sides by winning percentage|with a perfect winning record]], emerging victorious in all 11 matches.<ref name="BBC final" /> As a result, it was also the 14th occasion a club won the Champions League unbeaten, with Bayern the 10th club to do so, and the first since Manchester United in [[2007–08 UEFA Champions League|2007–08]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Menon |first=Anirudh |date=23 August 2020 |title=Bayern's record 11 and 35, Lewandowski first since Ronaldo: Best stats from UCL final vs. PSG |work=ESPN |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/uefa-champions-league/story/4165041/bayern-record-11-35-lewandowski-first-since-ronaldo-best-stats-champions-league-final-psg |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

With the win, six Bayern players secured their second European Cup title, five of which were from the club's victorious [[2012–13 UEFA Champions League|2012–13 Champions League campaign]]: David Alaba, Jérôme Boateng, [[Javi Martínez]], Thomas Müller and Manuel Neuer. Therefore, these five joined eight other players who have won two European trebles: [[Dani Alves]], [[Sergio Busquets]], [[Andrés Iniesta]], [[Lionel Messi]], [[Pedro (footballer, born 1987)|Pedro]], [[Gerard Piqué]] and [[Xavi (footballer, born 1980)|Xavi]] with Barcelona in 2008–09 and 2014–15, as well as [[Samuel Eto'o]] with Barcelona in 2008–09 and Inter Milan in [[2009–10 Inter Milan season|2009–10]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Kappel |first=David |date=24 August 2020 |title=All 13 Footballers Who've Won Two European Trebles |work=Soccer Laduma |url=https://www.soccerladuma.co.za/news/articles/international/categories/international/all-13-footballers-who-ve-won-two-european-trebles/684181 |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> [[Thiago Alcântara|Thiago]] was the only other Bayern player to have won the Champions League prior, doing so with Barcelona in [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League|2010–11]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Pérez |first=David |date=22 August 2020 |title=Flick's Bayern Munich following Heynckes' path |work=[[Marca (newspaper)|Marca]] |url=https://www.marca.com/en/football/international-football/2020/08/22/5f404ae622601d0a1a8b4606.html |access-date=23 August 2020}}</ref> Alphonso Davies became the first Canadian male international to win the Champions League.<ref>{{Cite news |date=23 August 2020 |title=Edmonton's Alphonso Davies wins Champions League title with Bayern Munich |work=CTV News Edmonton |url=https://www.ctvnews.ca/edmonton/article/edmontons-alphonso-davies-wins-champions-league-title-with-bayern-munich/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

Paris Saint-Germain's defeat meant that the past seven final debutants since the success of Borussia Dortmund in [[1997 UEFA Champions League final|1997]] had all lost, including fellow Ligue 1 club Monaco in 2004.<ref name="BBC final" /> This also meant their [[Le Classique|arch-rival]] Marseille were still the only French club to ever win the European Cup, having done so in 1993. The match was the lowest-scoring Champions League final since the goalless [[2003 UEFA Champions League final|2003 edition]] between Juventus and Milan and the first final to be ended 1–0 since Real Madrid's victory over Juventus in 1998.<ref name="RSSSF EC" />

Following the completion of the competition, UEFA's Technical Observers named nine Bayern Munich players in the Champions League squad of the season: David Alaba, Alphonso Davies, Serge Gnabry, Leon Goretzka, Joshua Kimmich, Robert Lewandowski, Thomas Müller, Manuel Neuer and Thiago. For Paris Saint-Germain, Marquinhos, Kylian Mbappé and Neymar were included in the squad.<ref>{{Cite news |date=28 August 2020 |title=UEFA Champions League Squad of the Season |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0260-103d6b6179e6-7a1932c7a58c-1000/ |access-date=28 August 2020}}</ref>

As the winners, Bayern also qualified to enter the group stage of the [[2020–21 UEFA Champions League]]; however as Bayern already qualified through their league performance, the berth reserved was given to the team that was top of the abandoned [[2019–20 Eredivisie]] ([[AFC Ajax|Ajax]]), the 11th-ranked association according to next season's access list.<ref name="uefa1819">{{Cite news |date=27 February 2018 |title=Champions League and Europa League changes next season |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0242-0e16a85014cb-5ffe48223b95-1000--champions-league-and-europa-league-changes-next-season/ |access-date=27 February 2018}}</ref>

Following the loss, Paris Saint-Germain fans clashed with [[riot police]] in [[Paris]], including on the [[Champs-Élysées]] and near the Parc des Princes.<ref>{{Cite news |date=23 August 2020 |title=Cars ignited, police attacked with fireworks as riots break out after PSG's CL defeat |work=[[Fox Sports (Australian TV network)|Fox Sports]] |url=https://www.foxsports.com.au/football/uefa-champions-league/cars-ignited-police-attacked-with-fireworks-as-riots-break-out-after-psgs-cl-defeat/news-story/8e1c88c9ff3f48d9799fe10c893fe19b |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> Cars were ignited, buildings were attacked, and flares and fireworks were shot at police after tear gas was used to disperse fans.<ref>{{Cite news |date=23 August 2020 |title=PSG fans clash with Paris riot police after CL defeat |work=[[USA Today]] |agency=[[Associated Press]] |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/2020/08/23/psg-fans-clash-with-paris-riot-police-after-cl-defeat/113458050/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

The two teams met again the following season at the quarter-final stage. The scores were level at 3–3 after the two legs, but Paris Saint-Germain won on away goals.

===Viewership===
In France, the final was televised on [[TF1]], and drew an average of 11.1&nbsp;million viewers, making it the third most watched Champions League final ever in France, after the 1993 and 2004 finals which also involved teams from the country. The game was also shown live on [[RMC (France)|RMC Sport]], who did not disclose their viewing figures. In Germany, the match drew a viewership of 12.8&nbsp;million, which was around 39.9% of the market share, on state-owned [[ZDF]], and another 1.04&nbsp;million viewers watching the match through pay-television broadcaster [[Sky Deutschland]].<ref name="SportBusiness">{{Cite news |last=Ross |first=Martin |date=24 August 2020 |title=TF1 nets peak-time record for PSG-Bayern final, ZDF audience nears 13m |work=SportBusiness |url=https://www.sportbusiness.com/news/tf1-nets-peak-time-record-for-psg-bayern-final-zdf-audience-nears-13m/ |access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref>

